A read of port 43h gives FFh. The control word is write only; the read went to a fourth channel past the
three, over the pic and set_irq pointers behind the array: with 4-byte pointers that channel's status_latched
is the top byte of set_irq, so IN AL,43h cleared it and the next timer interrupt jumped to a bad address.
Upstream: tiny386 ec16d43.

--- a/i8254.c
+++ b/i8254.c
@@ -235,6 +235,11 @@
 	PITChannelState *s;
 
 	addr &= 3;
+	/* 43h, the control word, is write only: the 8254 leaves the bus
+	   alone, and there is no fourth channel (channels[3] would be the
+	   pic and set_irq pointers after the array) */
+	if (addr == 3)
+		return 0xff;
 	s = &pit->channels[addr];
 	if (s->status_latched) {
 		s->status_latched = 0;
